Protocol Target Lab third-party source notes — HTTP, DNS, MQTT and Telnet

The project's original Rust manager, Web application, HTTP/DNS/MQTT/Telnet wrappers and test clients,
and integration scripts are distributed under the accompanying MIT LICENSE.
Third-party components retain their original licenses. Full license and copyright
texts for the locked Linux Rust dependency closure and bundled Web components
are in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.txt. The version/source/file index is
third-party/licenses.json (third-party-licenses.json in the installed docs).
The deb installs both files under /usr/share/doc/protocol-target-lab. Keep LICENSE,
this notice, the full license bundle and its index with standalone binaries.

The bundle includes nested upstream notices and the complete Vite notice file.
The include_dir crates omit their license text from their published archives;
third-party/upstream/include_dir preserves the license from the exact upstream
commit recorded in those archives. Generation and verification require only
prepared Cargo/npm caches; scripts/collect-third-party-licenses.py never downloads.

Rust dependencies:
  Cargo.toml lists direct dependencies. Cargo.lock records exact resolved package
  versions and registry checksums. Crate license declarations and upstream source
  metadata are available through `cargo metadata --locked --format-version 1`.
  Dependency source archives and LICENSE/NOTICE files must accompany any future
  complete offline source distribution; a Cargo lockfile alone is not that bundle.

Web dependencies:
  frontend/package.json lists direct dependencies. frontend/package-lock.json
  records package versions, source locations, integrity values and supplied license
  metadata. Preserve upstream license files when distributing dependency materials.

HTTP runtime image:
  Python 3.12, Python Software Foundation License, https://www.python.org/psf/license/
  Official Python image build sources: https://github.com/docker-library/python
  Debian base packages retain their package licenses in /usr/share/doc inside the
  image. images/http/base-image.lock identifies the selected public base digest.
  The worker uses only the Python standard library; no pip packages are installed.

Docker Engine and CLI:
  The manager invokes the system Docker CLI with structured arguments. The engine
  and its CLI are provided by the Ubuntu docker.io system package, not copied
  into this source repository or the manager deb. Upstream: https://github.com/moby/moby and
  https://github.com/docker/cli. Docker installation/dependency preparation is an
  administrator or offline-bundle preparation step, never a package-maintainer
  script download.

DNS runtime image:
  dnsmasq 2.90, copyright Simon Kelley and upstream contributors, is offered under
  GPL version 2 or (at your option) version 3. Complete corresponding unmodified
  source, both license texts, exact build recipes/flags and source SHA-256 are in
  third-party/dnsmasq and images/dns. The DNS image includes these materials at
  /usr/share/doc/protocol-target-lab-dns; scripts/dns-assets.sh exports them beside
  the saved runtime image. Keep them with redistributed DNS images. The MIT
  license of the original Python wrapper/client does not relicense dnsmasq.
  Python/Debian base components retain their own licenses as described above.
  dnsmasq is in the separately imported image, not the native manager deb.

MQTT runtime image:
  Eclipse Mosquitto 2.0.18 is distributed under the upstream choice of Eclipse
  Public License 2.0 or Eclipse Distribution License 1.0. Complete original
  source, documented upstream packet-size patches, both licenses and bundled
  uthash/utlist notices are preserved in
  third-party/mosquitto and in the runtime image. images/mqtt contains the exact
  build recipes and dependency/source lock. scripts/mqtt-assets.sh exports these
  materials beside the saved image. Mosquitto is not copied into the manager deb;
  original Python wrappers/clients use MIT and base components keep their licenses.

Telnet runtime image:
  telnetlib3 4.0.3 is ISC licensed; wcwidth 0.6.0 is MIT licensed. telnetlib3 also
  bundles a CPython telnetlib backport. Complete original CPython/Python license
  and provenance materials, upstream package source/wheels, package licenses and
  dependency hashes are preserved under third-party/telnetlib3 and in the image.
  images/telnet and scripts/telnet-assets.sh provide offline runtime build/export
  materials. These Python components run in the separate image, not in the native
  manager binary; the Rust/Web license bundle index describes its own closure.

IEC104 runtime image:
  c104 2.2.1 and its native lib60870 implementation are GPLv3 software. A documented
  project patch preserves incremental state when a TCP length byte has not yet
  arrived. It is not represented as an upstream backport. The full
  public source distribution includes native sources and bundled mbedtls/pybind11
  materials with their original notices. Pinned sources, wheel, build recipes
  and separate dependency provenance are in third-party/iec104 and images/iec104,
  and accompany the runtime/source export. These components run in the separate
  worker image, not in the native Rust/Web manager. Preserve upstream licenses
  and complete source/build materials when redistributing the image.

This notice describes implemented HTTP, DNS, MQTT, Telnet and IEC104 components. Other protocol
libraries and a complete offline distribution are not included or claimed here.
